(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State and prove the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus \(Parts 1 and 2\) using Riemann sums and the Mean Value Theorem for integrals.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Fundamental Theorem of Calculus - Part 1:) Tj
T*
(   - Statement: Let f: [a, b] -> R be continuous. Define F\(x\) = int_a^x f\(t\) dt for x in [a, b].) Tj
T*
(   Then F\(x\) is continuous on [a, b], differentiable on \(a, b\), and F'\(x\) = f\(x\) for all x in \(a,) Tj
T*
(   b\).) Tj
T*
(   - Proof:) Tj
T*
(   * By definition of derivative:) Tj
T*
(   F'\(x\) = lim_{h -> 0} [ F\(x + h\) - F\(x\) ] / h = lim_{h -> 0} \(1 / h\) * [ int_a^\(x+h\) f\(t\) dt -) Tj
T*
(   int_a^x f\(t\) dt ]) Tj
T*
(   = lim_{h -> 0} \(1 / h\) * int_x^\(x+h\) f\(t\) dt.) Tj
T*
(   * By the Mean Value Theorem for Definite Integrals, since f is continuous on [x, x + h], there) Tj
T*
(   exists c_h between x and x + h such that:) Tj
T*
(   int_x^\(x+h\) f\(t\) dt = f\(c_h\) * [ \(x + h\) - x ] = f\(c_h\) * h.) Tj
T*
(   * Substitute this into the derivative limit:) Tj
T*
(   F'\(x\) = lim_{h -> 0} \(1 / h\) * [ f\(c_h\) * h ] = lim_{h -> 0} f\(c_h\).) Tj
T*
(   * As h -> 0, since x <= c_h <= x + h, by the squeeze theorem c_h -> x.) Tj
T*
(   * Since f is continuous at x: lim_{c_h -> x} f\(c_h\) = f\(x\).) Tj
T*
(   * Therefore: F'\(x\) = f\(x\). \(Proved Part 1\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Fundamental Theorem of Calculus - Part 2 \(Evaluation Theorem\):) Tj
T*
(   - Statement: If f is continuous on [a, b] and G is any antiderivative of f on [a, b] \(i.e.,) Tj
T*
(   G'\(x\) = f\(x\)\), then:) Tj
T*
(   int_a^b f\(x\) dx = G\(b\) - G\(a\).) Tj
T*
(   - Proof:) Tj
T*
(   * Let F\(x\) = int_a^x f\(t\) dt. By Part 1, F'\(x\) = f\(x\).) Tj
T*
(   * Since G'\(x\) = f\(x\) as well, \(F - G\)'\(x\) = F'\(x\) - G'\(x\) = f\(x\) - f\(x\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   * A function whose derivative is identically zero on an interval is constant:) Tj
T*
(   F\(x\) - G\(x\) = C => F\(x\) = G\(x\) + C.) Tj
T*
(   * Evaluate at x = a:) Tj
T*
(   F\(a\) = int_a^a f\(t\) dt = 0 => 0 = G\(a\) + C => C = -G\(a\).) Tj
T*
(   * Therefore: F\(x\) = G\(x\) - G\(a\).) Tj
T*
(   * Now evaluate at x = b:) Tj
T*
(   F\(b\) = int_a^b f\(t\) dt = G\(b\) - G\(a\).) Tj
T*
(   * Hence: int_a^b f\(x\) dx = G\(b\) - G\(a\). \(Proved Part 2\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the reduction formula for I_n = int sin^n x dx. Hence, deduce Wallis' Formula for int_0^\(pi/2\) sin^n x dx for both even and odd positive integers n.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Derivation of Reduction Formula:) Tj
T*
(   - Let I_n = int sin^n x dx = int sin^\(n-1\) x * sin x dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Integrate by parts: let u = sin^\(n-1\) x and dv = sin x dx.) Tj
T*
(   du = \(n - 1\) * sin^\(n-2\) x * cos x dx) Tj
T*
(   v = -cos x.) Tj
T*
(   - I_n = u * v - int v du) Tj
T*
(   = -sin^\(n-1\) x * cos x - int \(-cos x\) * [ \(n - 1\) * sin^\(n-2\) x * cos x ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = -sin^\(n-1\) x * cos x + \(n - 1\) * int sin^\(n-2\) x * cos^2 x dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Use identity cos^2 x = 1 - sin^2 x:) Tj
T*
(   I_n = -sin^\(n-1\) x * cos x + \(n - 1\) * int sin^\(n-2\) x * \(1 - sin^2 x\) dx) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
