(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Derivation of Reduction Formula:) Tj
T*
(   - Let I_{m,n} = int x^m * \(ln x\)^n dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Integrate by parts: let u = \(ln x\)^n, dv = x^m dx.) Tj
T*
(   du = n * \(ln x\)^\(n-1\) * \(1 / x\) dx, v = x^\(m+1\) / \(m + 1\).) Tj
T*
(   - I_{m,n} = [ x^\(m+1\) * \(ln x\)^n ] / \(m + 1\) - int [ x^\(m+1\) / \(m + 1\) ] * [ n * \(ln x\)^\(n-1\) /) Tj
T*
(   x ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = [ x^\(m+1\) * \(ln x\)^n ] / \(m + 1\) - [ n / \(m + 1\) ] * int x^m * \(ln x\)^\(n-1\) dx) Tj
T*
(   = [ x^\(m+1\) * \(ln x\)^n ] / \(m + 1\) - [ n / \(m + 1\) ] * I_{m, n-1}. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Definite Integral over [0, 1]:) Tj
T*
(   - Boundary term: [ x^\(m+1\) * \(ln x\)^n / \(m + 1\) ]_0^1:) Tj
T*
(   At x = 1: ln\(1\) = 0 => 0.) Tj
T*
(   At x -> 0+: By L'Hopital's rule, lim_{x -> 0+} x^\(m+1\) * \(ln x\)^n = 0 \(for m + 1 > 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Thus, the boundary term vanishes completely at both limits.) Tj
T*
(   - Recurrence relation for definite integral J_{m,n} = int_0^1 x^m * \(ln x\)^n dx:) Tj
T*
(   J_{m,n} = - [ n / \(m + 1\) ] * J_{m, n-1}.) Tj
T*
(   - Repeated application gives:) Tj
T*
(   J_{m,n} = \(-1\)^n * [ n! / \(m + 1\)^n ] * J_{m, 0}.) Tj
T*
(   - Base integral J_{m, 0}:) Tj
T*
(   J_{m, 0} = int_0^1 x^m dx = [ x^\(m+1\) / \(m + 1\) ]_0^1 = 1 / \(m + 1\).) Tj
T*
(   - General formula:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^1 x^m * \(ln x\)^n dx = \(-1\)^n * [ n! / \(m + 1\)^\(n + 1\) ].) Tj
T*
(   3. Evaluation for m = 3, n = 2:) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute m = 3, n = 2 into the general formula:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^1 x^3 * \(ln x\)^2 dx = \(-1\)^2 * [ 2! / \(3 + 1\)^\(2 + 1\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = 1 * [ 2 / 4^3 ]) Tj
T*
(   = 2 / 64 = 1 / 32.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^1 x^3 * \(ln x\)^2 dx = 1 / 32.) Tj
