(   [Problem Statement]: Define the Gamma function Gamma\(n\). Prove that the integral converges for all n > 0. Establish the recurrence relation Gamma\(n + 1\) = n * Gamma\(n\) and deduce that Gamma\(n + 1\) = n! for non-negative integers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Convergence:) Tj
T*
(   - Definition: The Gamma function is defined by the improper Euler integral of the second kind:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(n\) = int_0^infinity e^\(-x\) * x^\(n - 1\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Convergence Proof:) Tj
T*
(   Split the integral into two parts:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(n\) = int_0^1 e^\(-x\) * x^\(n - 1\) dx + int_1^infinity e^\(-x\) * x^\(n - 1\) dx = I1 + I2.) Tj
T*
(   * Convergence of I1 \(near x = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   For x in \(0, 1], e^\(-x\) <= 1, so 0 < e^\(-x\) * x^\(n - 1\) <= x^\(n - 1\).) Tj
T*
(   int_0^1 x^\(n - 1\) dx = [ x^n / n ]_0^1 = 1 / n \(finite iff n > 0\).) Tj
T*
(   By the direct comparison test, I1 converges for all n > 0.) Tj
T*
(   * Convergence of I2 \(as x -> infinity\):) Tj
T*
(   By Taylor's expansion of e^x, for any positive integer k > n, e^x > x^k / k! for large x.) Tj
T*
(   Thus: e^\(-x\) * x^\(n - 1\) < k! * x^\(n - 1 - k\) = k! / x^\(k - n + 1\).) Tj
T*
(   Choosing k large enough so that k - n + 1 > 1, the integral int_1^infinity \(1 / x^\(k - n + 1\)\)) Tj
T*
(   dx converges by the p-integral test.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore, I2 converges for all real n.) Tj
T*
(   * Hence, Gamma\(n\) converges for all n > 0.) Tj
T*
(   2. Derivation of Recurrence Relation Gamma\(n + 1\) = n * Gamma\(n\):) Tj
T*
(   - Gamma\(n + 1\) = int_0^infinity e^\(-x\) * x^n dx.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
