(   [Problem Statement]: Prove that Gamma\(1/2\) = sqrt\(pi\) by transforming the product of two Gaussian integrals into polar coordinates.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Express Gamma\(1/2\) as an integral:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(n\) = int_0^infinity e^\(-t\) * t^\(n - 1\) dt.) Tj
T*
(   For n = 1/2:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(1/2\) = int_0^infinity e^\(-t\) * t^\(-1/2\) dt.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Substitute t = x^2:) Tj
T*
(   dt = 2x dx => t^\(-1/2\) dt = \(1/x\) * 2x dx = 2 dx.) Tj
T*
(   Limits: when t = 0, x = 0; when t -> infinity, x -> infinity.) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(1/2\) = int_0^infinity e^\(-x^2\) * 2 dx = 2 * int_0^infinity e^\(-x^2\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Consider the square [Gamma\(1/2\)]^2:) Tj
T*
(   Write as a product of two independent single integrals with dummy variables x and y:) Tj
T*
(   [Gamma\(1/2\)]^2 = [ 2 * int_0^infinity e^\(-x^2\) dx ] * [ 2 * int_0^infinity e^\(-y^2\) dy ]) Tj
T*
(   = 4 * int_0^infinity int_0^infinity e^\(-\(x^2 + y^2\)\) dx dy.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Transform to Polar Coordinates \(x = r*cos theta, y = r*sin theta\):) Tj
T*
(   - Area element: dx dy = r dr dtheta.) Tj
T*
(   - x^2 + y^2 = r^2.) Tj
T*
(   - Region of integration: the entire first quadrant \(x >= 0, y >= 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Therefore: r ranges from 0 to infinity; theta ranges from 0 to pi/2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Evaluate the Double Integral:) Tj
T*
(   [Gamma\(1/2\)]^2 = 4 * int_0^\(pi/2\) dtheta * int_0^infinity e^\(-r^2\) * r dr.) Tj
T*
(   - Angular integral: int_0^\(pi/2\) dtheta = [ theta ]_0^\(pi/2\) = pi / 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Radial integral: int_0^infinity e^\(-r^2\) * r dr:) Tj
T*
(   Substitute u = r^2, du = 2r dr => r dr = du / 2:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^infinity e^\(-u\) * \(du / 2\) = \(1/2\) * [-e^\(-u\)]_0^infinity = \(1/2\) * \(0 - \(-1\)\) = 1/2.) Tj
T*
(   - Combine terms:) Tj
T*
(   [Gamma\(1/2\)]^2 = 4 * \(pi / 2\) * \(1 / 2\) = pi.) Tj
T*
(   Step 6: Take the positive square root:) Tj
T*
(   Since the integrand e^\(-t\) * t^\(-1/2\) > 0 on \(0, infinity\), Gamma\(1/2\) must be positive:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(1/2\) = sqrt\(pi\).) Tj
T*
(   Hence proved.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
