(   [Problem Statement]: Define the Beta function Beta\(m, n\). Prove: \(i\) Beta\(m, n\) = Beta\(n, m\) \(symmetry\), and \(ii\) Beta\(m, n\) = 2 * int_0^\(pi/2\) sin^\(2m-1\) theta * cos^\(2n-1\) theta dtheta \(trigonometric form\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition:) Tj
T*
(   The Beta function \(Euler's integral of the first kind\) is defined for m > 0, n > 0 by:) Tj
T*
(   Beta\(m, n\) = int_0^1 x^\(m - 1\) * \(1 - x\)^\(n - 1\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof of Symmetry: Beta\(m, n\) = Beta\(n, m\):) Tj
T*
(   - In Beta\(m, n\) = int_0^1 x^\(m - 1\) * \(1 - x\)^\(n - 1\) dx, substitute x = 1 - t:) Tj
T*
(   dx = -dt.) Tj
T*
(   Limits: when x = 0, t = 1; when x = 1, t = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Beta\(m, n\) = int_1^0 \(1 - t\)^\(m - 1\) * t^\(n - 1\) * \(-dt\)) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^1 t^\(n - 1\) * \(1 - t\)^\(m - 1\) dt.) Tj
T*
(   - Since t is a dummy variable, this is identically Beta\(n, m\):) Tj
T*
(   Beta\(m, n\) = Beta\(n, m\). \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Proof of Trigonometric Form:) Tj
T*
(   - In Beta\(m, n\) = int_0^1 x^\(m - 1\) * \(1 - x\)^\(n - 1\) dx, substitute x = sin^2 theta:) Tj
T*
(   dx = 2 * sin theta * cos theta dtheta.) Tj
T*
(   1 - x = 1 - sin^2 theta = cos^2 theta.) Tj
T*
(   Limits: when x = 0, theta = 0; when x = 1, theta = pi / 2.) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute into integral:) Tj
T*
(   Beta\(m, n\) = int_0^\(pi/2\) \(sin^2 theta\)^\(m - 1\) * \(cos^2 theta\)^\(n - 1\) * \(2 * sin theta * cos) Tj
T*
(   theta\) dtheta) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^\(pi/2\) sin^\(2m - 2\) theta * cos^\(2n - 2\) theta * 2 * sin theta * cos theta dtheta) Tj
T*
(   = 2 * int_0^\(pi/2\) sin^\(2m - 1\) theta * cos^\(2n - 1\) theta dtheta. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   4. Direct Corollary for Wallis' Integrals:) Tj
T*
(   Dividing by 2 gives:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^\(pi/2\) sin^p theta * cos^q theta dtheta = \(1/2\) * Beta\(\(p + 1\)/2, \(q + 1\)/2\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
