(   [Problem Statement]: Prove the fundamental relation connecting Beta and Gamma functions: Beta\(m, n\) = [ Gamma\(m\) * Gamma\(n\) ] / Gamma\(m + n\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Express Gamma\(m\) and Gamma\(n\) in transformed variable form:) Tj
T*
(   From Subjective 9, substitute t = x^2 into Gamma\(m\):) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(m\) = 2 * int_0^infinity e^\(-x^2\) * x^\(2m - 1\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   Similarly, using variable y:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(n\) = 2 * int_0^infinity e^\(-y^2\) * y^\(2n - 1\) dy.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Form the product Gamma\(m\) * Gamma\(n\):) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(m\) * Gamma\(n\) = [ 2 * int_0^infinity e^\(-x^2\) * x^\(2m - 1\) dx ] * [ 2 * int_0^infinity) Tj
T*
(   e^\(-y^2\) * y^\(2n - 1\) dy ]) Tj
T*
(   = 4 * int_0^infinity int_0^infinity e^\(-\(x^2 + y^2\)\) * x^\(2m - 1\) * y^\(2n - 1\) dx dy.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Convert to Polar Coordinates \(x = r*cos theta, y = r*sin theta\):) Tj
T*
(   - x^2 + y^2 = r^2.) Tj
T*
(   - dx dy = r dr dtheta.) Tj
T*
(   - Limits: r in [0, infinity\), theta in [0, pi/2] \(first quadrant\).) Tj
T*
(   - x^\(2m - 1\) * y^\(2n - 1\) = \(r * cos theta\)^\(2m - 1\) * \(r * sin theta\)^\(2n - 1\)) Tj
T*
(   = r^\(2m + 2n - 2\) * cos^\(2n - 1\) theta * sin^\(2m - 1\) theta.) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute into product:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(m\) * Gamma\(n\) = 4 * int_0^\(pi/2\) [ sin^\(2m - 1\) theta * cos^\(2n - 1\) theta ] dtheta *) Tj
T*
(   int_0^infinity e^\(-r^2\) * r^\(2m + 2n - 1\) dr.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Factor into two independent integrals:) Tj
T*
(   Gamma\(m\) * Gamma\(n\) = [ 2 * int_0^\(pi/2\) sin^\(2m - 1\) theta * cos^\(2n - 1\) theta dtheta ] * [ 2) Tj
T*
(   * int_0^infinity e^\(-r^2\) * r^\(2\(m+n\) - 1\) dr ].) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Identify each factor:) Tj
T*
