(   [Problem Statement]: Prove that int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin x\) dx = - \(pi / 2\) * ln 2 using properties of definite integrals.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Define the integral:) Tj
T*
(   Let I = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin x\) dx. \(Eq. 1\)) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Apply the property int_0^a f\(x\) dx = int_0^a f\(a - x\) dx:) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin\(pi/2 - x\)\) dx) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(cos x\) dx. \(Eq. 2\)) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Add Eq. 1 and Eq. 2:) Tj
T*
(   2*I = int_0^\(pi/2\) [ ln\(sin x\) + ln\(cos x\) ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin x * cos x\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Use identity sin x * cos x = \(sin 2x\) / 2:) Tj
T*
(   2*I = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln [ \(sin 2x\) / 2 ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^\(pi/2\) [ ln\(sin 2x\) - ln 2 ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin 2x\) dx - ln 2 * int_0^\(pi/2\) 1 dx) Tj
T*
(   = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin 2x\) dx - \(pi / 2\) * ln 2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Evaluate J = int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin 2x\) dx:) Tj
T*
(   Substitute 2x = t => dx = dt / 2.) Tj
T*
(   When x = 0, t = 0; when x = pi/2, t = pi:) Tj
T*
(   J = \(1/2\) * int_0^pi ln\(sin t\) dt.) Tj
T*
(   Since sin\(pi - t\) = sin\(t\), use int_0^\(2a\) f\(t\) dt = 2 * int_0^a f\(t\) dt:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^pi ln\(sin t\) dt = 2 * int_0^\(pi/2\) ln\(sin t\) dt = 2 * I.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore:) Tj
T*
(   J = \(1/2\) * \(2 * I\) = I.) Tj
T*
(   Step 6: Substitute J = I back into the equation:) Tj
T*
(   2*I = I - \(pi / 2\) * ln 2) Tj
T*
(   => 2*I - I = - \(pi / 2\) * ln 2) Tj
T*
(   => I = - \(pi / 2\) * ln 2.) Tj
T*
(   Hence proved.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
