(>> Solution to Question 16:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Prove that int_0^pi [ x * sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx = pi^2 / 4.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Define the integral:) Tj
T*
(   Let I = int_0^pi [ x * sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx. \(Eq. 1\)) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Apply the property int_0^a f\(x\) dx = int_0^a f\(a - x\) dx:) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^pi [ \(pi - x\) * sin\(pi - x\) / \(1 + cos^2\(pi - x\)\) ] dx.) Tj
T*
(   Since sin\(pi - x\) = sin x and cos\(pi - x\) = -cos x \(so cos^2\(pi - x\) = cos^2 x\):) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^pi [ \(pi - x\) * sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx) Tj
T*
(   = pi * int_0^pi [ sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx - int_0^pi [ x * sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Notice the second integral is I:) Tj
T*
(   I = pi * int_0^pi [ sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx - I) Tj
T*
(   => 2*I = pi * int_0^pi [ sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Evaluate the remaining integral:) Tj
T*
(   Let K = int_0^pi [ sin x / \(1 + cos^2 x\) ] dx.) Tj
T*
(   Substitute u = cos x => du = -sin x dx.) Tj
T*
(   When x = 0, u = 1; when x = pi, u = -1:) Tj
T*
(   K = int_1^\(-1\) [ -du / \(1 + u^2\) ] = int_{-1}^1 [ du / \(1 + u^2\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = [ arctan\(u\) ]_{-1}^1 = arctan\(1\) - arctan\(-1\)) Tj
T*
(   = \(pi / 4\) - \(-pi / 4\) = pi / 2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Solve for I:) Tj
T*
(   2*I = pi * K = pi * \(pi / 2\) = pi^2 / 2) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
