BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the Disk Method for the volume of a solid of revolution. Use it to prove that the volume of a sphere of radius R is \(4/3\) * pi * R^3.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Derivation of Disk Method:) Tj
T*
(   - Let region under y = f\(x\) from x = a to x = b be revolved 360 degrees about the x-axis.) Tj
T*
(   - Slice the resulting solid into n thin vertical circular disks of thickness Delta_x_i.) Tj
T*
(   - The radius of disk i is y_i = f\(x_i\).) Tj
T*
(   - Volume of an infinitesimal circular disk:) Tj
T*
(   Delta_V_i = pi * \(radius\)^2 * thickness = pi * y_i^2 * Delta_x_i.) Tj
T*
(   - Total volume V is the limit of Riemann sums as Delta_x -> 0:) Tj
T*
(   V = int_a^b pi * y^2 dx = pi * int_a^b [f\(x\)]^2 dx. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof for Volume of a Sphere of Radius R:) Tj
T*
(   - A sphere of radius R is generated by revolving the circle x^2 + y^2 = R^2 about the x-axis.) Tj
T*
(   - Bounding curve: y^2 = R^2 - x^2.) Tj
T*
(   - Domain: x ranges from -R to R.) Tj
T*
(   - Volume formula:) Tj
T*
(   V = pi * int_{-R}^R y^2 dx = pi * int_{-R}^R \(R^2 - x^2\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Since R^2 - x^2 is an even function:) Tj
T*
(   V = 2 * pi * int_0^R \(R^2 - x^2\) dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Evaluate integral:) Tj
T*
(   V = 2 * pi * [ R^2 * x - x^3 / 3 ]_0^R) Tj
T*
(   = 2 * pi * [ R^3 - R^3 / 3 ]) Tj
T*
(   = 2 * pi * [ \(2/3\) * R^3 ]) Tj
T*
(   = \(4/3\) * pi * R^3.) Tj
T*
(   Hence proved.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
endobj
